1 NAME Gladys Jennie /Friestad/
2 GIVN Gladys Jennie
2 SURN Friestad
2 _MARNM Nelson
1 SEX F
1 BURI
2 PLAC Tell Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY Kintyre
3 STAE ND
1 BIRT
2 DATE 17 MAR 1909
2 ADDR
3 STAE North Dakota
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1 JUN 1997
2 ADDR
3 CITY Amarillo
3 STAE Texas
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Baptised: May 16, 1909, Glenwood Lutheran Church, Crosby Co., ND [''U.S., Evangelical Lutheran Church of America, Records, 1875-1940'']
2 CONT
2 CONT US Census 1910 (1 year old): https://familysearch.org/pal:/MM9.1.1/MLGC-7JR - "Gladys I Friestad"
2 CONT
2 CONT US Census 1930 (21 years old, still living at home): https://familysearch.org/pal:/MM9.1.1/XKVW-44T - "Gladis J Friestad".
2 CONT
2 CONT Gladys Friestad married Oscar Fredolph (Fred) Nelson on March 31, 1934.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lived in 1940: near Kintyre, Emmons Co., ND (''Bismarck Tribune'', October 25, 1940, p. 10)
2 CONT
2 CONT Kroeber, North Dakota.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reside on the Nelson Bros. Ranch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fred was born Sept. 3, 1895 to Oscar and Mathilda Nelson, pioneer settlers, on a farm near the old Tell Post Office. He grew up on the farm where he and his family still live. Gladys Friestad attended Kintyre High School during he
2 CONC r freshman year and lived with the Gustav Swanson family in the house where David Kuiper now lives. Mrs. Swanson was a sister to O.W. Nelson who was Fred's father. Others in her class were Grace Olson Ohm, Ethel Hansen, Dale Rambo
2 CONC ugh, Ellen Oberg, and Elma Barta. She spent the next two years at Oak Grove High School in Fargo where she was graduated. She then attended Valley City Teacher's College for two years. She taught school for six years before she an
2 CONC d Fred Nelson were married in 1934. Among her pupils, Gladys taught Norman and Mildred Dickson, Lars and Sigrid Storhaug, Orville, Alvin and Alice Stanley, Sigfried and Signor Friestad and the Kraft children who lived on the Shafe
2 CONC r farm for a time. Gladys and Fred recently celebrated their 45th wedding anniversary at the Lutheran Church in Kintyre. They have six children.  [''Kintyre, North Dakota 75th Anniversary, 1904-1979'', http://www.rootsweb.ancestry
2 CONC .com/~ndemmons/historykintyre.pdf]
2 CONT
2 CONT Fred Nelson was born September 3, 1895, to Oscar and Mathidla Nelson. He was educated at Tell School where he completed 8th grade. He grew up on the Nelson farm which he operated together with his brothers Victor and Hilding. Fred
2 CONC  married Gladys Friestad, daughter of Ole and Olena Friestad, on March 31, 1934. They continued to farm and raised six children: Olive (1935); Earl (1938), Delores (1939), Wallace (1940), Gary (1943), and Joyce (1949). Fred died F
2 CONC eb. 6, 1973. He is buried in Tell Cemetery. Gladys has lived at Central Dakota Nursing Home in Jamestown since July 1982. The Nelson farm is now operated by Earl and Wallace and their families. [''Braddock, ND, 1884-1984'', http:/
2 CONC /www.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~ndemmons/historybraddock1884-1984.pdf]
2 CONT
2 CONT Death notice: FRIESTAD (NELSON), Gladys J.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Birth - 03/17/1909
2 CONT * Death - 06/01/1997
2 CONT * Age - 88
2 CONT * Source - ''Bismarck Tribune'', 06/03/1997
2 CONT
2 CONT JAMESTOWN -- Gladys J. Nelson, 88, Jamestown, died June 1, 1997, in a Jamestown nursing home. Services will be held at 2 p.m. Thursday at Trinity Lutheran Church, Kintyre, with burial in Tell Cemetery, Kintyre.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is survived by three daughters, Olive Jacobson, Braddock, Dolores Kounovsky, Longmont, Colo., and Joyce Mueller, Jamestown; and three sons: Earl, Braddock; Wallace, Amarillo, Texas; and Gary, El Dorado Hills, Calif. (Dahlstrom
2 CONC  Funeral Home, Napoleon) 06/04/1997
2 CONT
2 CONT JAMESTOWN -- Gladys J. Nelson, 88, Jamestown, formerly of Braddock, died June 1, 1997, in a Jamestown nursing home. Services will be held at 2 p.m. Thursday at Trinity Lutheran Church, Kintyre, with the Revs. Gary Connors-Nelson a
2 CONC nd Cathy Connors-Nelson officiating. Helen Roth will serve as organist, with special music provided by Marjo Headland and DyVonne Nelson. Burial will be in Tell Cemetery, rural Kintyre. Visitation will be 9 a.m.-8 p.m. today and 9
2 CONC  a.m.-noon Thursday at Dahlstrom Funeral Home, Napoleon, and will continue at the church one hour before services.  Pallbearers for the service are Doug Friestad, Soren Friestad, Rolf Svanes, Robert Weiss, Curtis Stanley and Tom S
2 CONC tanley.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gladys was born March 17, 1909, at Wildrose, the daughter of Ole and Olena (Westley) Friestad. As a young girl she moved with her parents to a farm at rural Kintyre. She grew up there and received her elementary education in rural
2 CONC  Kintyre schools. Gladys went on to attend Oak Grove High School in Fargo, graduating in 1925. Gladys continued her education and attended Valley City Teachers College and received her degree in 1927. Gladys taught for a number of
2 CONC  years in several North Dakota communities until her marriage to Fred Nelson March 31, 1934, at Valley City. They returned to the Nelson family farm near Braddock, where they lived until 1982. She then entered Central Dakota Villa
2 CONC ge Nursing Home at Jamestown. Fred preceded her in death on Feb. 6, 1983. Gladys was a member of Zion Lutheran Church at Tell, and served as the church organist for many years.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is survived by three daughters and sons-in-law, Olive and Richard Jacobson, Braddock, DoLores and Richard Kounovsky, Longmont, Colo., and Joyce and Tom Mueller, Jamestown; three sons and two daughters-in-law, Earl and Eunice, 
2 CONC Braddock, Wallace and DyVonne, Amarillo, Texas, and Gary, El Dorado Hills, Calif.; 15 grandchildren; and five great-grandchildren. She was preceded in death by her husband; three brothers; and one sister. Gladys was the last of he
2 CONC r immediate siblings. [http://web.archive.org/web/20010427050853/http://www.heier.net/obits/obit110.html#bm7945]
